# Commit 6: Fix orphaned Start/End events in zero-component flows

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: same as commits 1-5 -- written for both future Claude sessions
and human contributors, explaining the evidence and reasoning behind each
decision.

---

## 1. What broke

A flow with **zero intermediate components** -- `sender -> receiver`
directly -- compiled successfully via `compileToZip()` and `validate()`
reported `valid: true`, but SAP Integration Suite rejected the resulting
`.iflw` on import with:

> "Start event should have an outgoing sequence flow."

This affected every "no intermediate steps" flow, but was most visible with
RFC: since RFC has no component representation at all (it's purely the
flow's receiver adapter -- see commit 4/5), a flow like "receive an employee
ID over HTTPS and send it to SAP via RFC" has genuinely **zero** entries in
`flow.getComponents()`, making this the easiest shape to hit by accident.

## 2. Root cause

`BpmnProcessMapper.map()` built the Start→...→End sequence-flow chain in
two places, each gated by `if (components.length > 0)`:

```typescript
// Start event to first component
if (components.length > 0) {
    process.flows.push(new BpmnSequenceFlow("SequenceFlow_3", "StartEvent_2", components[0].id));
}
...
// Last component to end event
if (components.length > 0) {
    process.flows.push(new BpmnSequenceFlow(`SequenceFlow_${connections.length + 4}`, lastComponent.id, "EndEvent_2"));
}
```

When `components.length === 0`, **both** blocks were skipped entirely --
nothing ever connected `StartEvent_2` to `EndEvent_2`. `StartEvent_2` ended
up with zero `<bpmn2:outgoing>` sequence flows and `EndEvent_2` with zero
`<bpmn2:incoming>` ones: two disconnected, orphaned events in an otherwise
structurally-plausible `.iflw`. `compileToZip()` doesn't validate BPMN graph
connectivity -- it only serializes whatever IR it's given -- so packaging
succeeded while the artifact itself was invalid. `validate()` had no check
for this either (it checks component-level concerns like router routes and
duplicate IDs, not the base Start/End skeleton every flow always has), so it
reported `valid: true` for a flow SAP would reject.

This also confirmed the task's suspicion directly: `connections: [{ from:
"sender", to: "receiver" }]` is filtered out by `fromJson()` before it ever
becomes an internal `Connection` object (it's adapter-level metadata,
matching `flow.getSender()`/`getReceiver()`), so it was never the thing
responsible for the internal Start→End wiring in the first place -- that
was always meant to be the mapper's job, and the mapper simply never did it
for the zero-component case.

## 3. The fix

Added an `else` branch to the first gate only (the second gate needs no
change -- it's naturally skipped when there are zero components, and
correctly still fires whenever there are one or more):

```typescript
if (components.length > 0) {
    process.flows.push(new BpmnSequenceFlow("SequenceFlow_3", "StartEvent_2", components[0].id));
} else {
    // Zero intermediate components: connect Start directly to End so the
    // process graph is always closed.
    process.flows.push(new BpmnSequenceFlow("SequenceFlow_3", "StartEvent_2", "EndEvent_2"));
}
```

This is the smallest change that closes the graph in every case, and
reuses the exact same `BpmnSequenceFlow` IR class and `SequenceFlow_3` id
slot the one-or-more-components path already used for the equivalent edge
-- no new writer, no new IR type, no change to how any component or adapter
is represented.

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**Modified**:
- `src/mapper/BpmnProcessMapper.ts` -- the fix described above (one `else`
  branch, ~15 lines including comments).

**New**:
- `test-sequence-flow-regression.ts`

**Deliberately not touched**: the RFC adapter/receiver architecture
(commits 4-5, confirmed still working and unmodified), JDBC, Process
Direct, HTTP/HTTPS, the Fiori UI, the CAP controller/`downloadFlow`
handling, and any AI/system-prompt logic (all outside this package's scope
per this task's explicit instruction). No writer, IR class, or validation
rule beyond the one mapper change was touched.

---

## 5. Testing

**Verified the fix is real, not just "tests pass"**: before finalizing,
temporarily reverted the mapper change (`git stash`) and re-ran the new
test suite against the unfixed code -- it failed exactly the two
zero-component scenarios ([1] HTTPS→RFC and [5]/[6] HTTPS→HTTPS) with
"StartEvent_2 has an outgoing sequence flow" / "EndEvent_2 is actually
reachable from StartEvent_2", while `validate()` and `compileToZip()` both
still reported success -- reproducing the exact reported bug and confirming
the test would have caught it. Restored the fix and re-ran; all scenarios
pass.

`test-sequence-flow-regression.ts`, 6 scenarios (the 5 required + one
extra covering the literal `connections: [{ from: "sender", to: "receiver"
}]` shape called out in the task), each checked against the **raw
generated `.iflw` XML** by parsing every `<bpmn2:sequenceFlow>` edge and
verifying:
- `validate()` succeeds
- `compileToZip()` produces a non-empty ZIP and the `.iflw` exists in it
- `StartEvent_2` has at least one outgoing sequence flow (not an orphan)
- `EndEvent_2` has at least one incoming sequence flow (not an orphan)
- `EndEvent_2` is **actually reachable** from `StartEvent_2` by following
  the sequence-flow graph (a stronger check than "has some edge from
  somewhere" -- confirms the graph is genuinely connected end-to-end, not
  just locally non-empty)

Scenarios: (1) HTTPS→RFC [zero components -- the reported bug], (2)
HTTPS→ContentModifier→RFC [one component], (3)
HTTPS→ContentModifier→ContentModifier→RFC [multiple components], (4)
HTTPS→ContentModifier→HTTPS [one component, non-RFC], (5) HTTPS→HTTPS [zero
components, non-RFC], (6) explicit `sender -> receiver` connection entry
[zero components].

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run after this change -- zero
regressions**: `test-rfc-regression.ts` (all 15 scenarios, confirming RFC's
architecture is completely unaffected), `test-jdbc-regression.ts`,
`test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`, `test-naming-version-regression.ts`,
`test-customer-flow-regression.ts`, `test-processdirect-regression.ts`,
`test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts`, `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts`
(9/9), `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` (3/3).

Run with (from `packages/compiler/`):
```
npm run build   # or: npx tsc --noEmit
npx ts-node test-sequence-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-rfc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-naming-version-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-customer-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-processdirect-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-rt-003-tests.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts
```

---

## 6. Example generated sequence for HTTPS -> RFC

```
Sequence flows:
  SequenceFlow_3: StartEvent_2 -> EndEvent_2

<bpmn2:startEvent id="StartEvent_2" name="Start">
    ...
    <bpmn2:outgoing>SequenceFlow_3</bpmn2:outgoing>
    <bpmn2:messageEventDefinition/>
</bpmn2:startEvent>

<bpmn2:endEvent id="EndEvent_2" name="End">
    ...
    <bpmn2:incoming>SequenceFlow_3</bpmn2:incoming>
    <bpmn2:messageEventDefinition/>
</bpmn2:endEvent>
```

`StartEvent_2` now has a real `<bpmn2:outgoing>`, `EndEvent_2` a real
`<bpmn2:incoming>`, both referencing `SequenceFlow_3`, which itself connects
them directly (`sourceRef="StartEvent_2" targetRef="EndEvent_2"`). No orphan
in either direction.

---

## 7. Known limitations

None identified for this specific fix -- it closes the graph unconditionally
whenever there are zero components, which is the only case that was
previously broken. Flows with one or more components were already correct
and remain byte-for-byte unchanged (verified via the full regression re-run
above).
